  "worked_solution": "We have a population of size $N=180,$ and $p=0.45$ represents the proportion of the population with the characteristic. \n\nTherefore,\n\n- the number of population members *with* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nNp=180(0.45)=81,\n$$\n\n- the number of population members *without* the characteristic equals \n\n$$\nN(1-p)=180(1-0.45)=99.\n$$\n\nWe select two members of the population at random. We need to find $P({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1),$ the probability that the second member has the characteristic given that the first member also has the characteristic.\n\nIf the first member has the characteristic, we have\n\n$\\,$ $N-1=180-1=179$ members remaining, and\n\n$\\,$ $Np-1=81-1=80$ members remaining that have the characteristic.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP({X}_{2}=1|{X}_{1}=1)=\\frac{Np-1}{N-1}=\\frac{80}{179}.\n$$",
